---
/**
 * /typesafe-jev/open-source — the question people ask when a hosted API is in their critical path:
 * what can I run myself, and what do I give up.
 *
 * Third page of the explainer cluster. Unlike the other two this one is written almost entirely
 * from first-hand reading: we opened the repositories, read the READMEs, the licences and the
 * evaluation tables, and quoted the publishers' own numbers rather than repeating coverage. The
 * one thing it refuses to do is pretend a 395M-parameter substitute is equivalent — the publisher
 * of the closest one publishes a head-to-head against the model it replaces, and it loses.
 */
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
import System from "../../../layouts/System.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import JsonLd from "../../../components/JsonLd.astro";
import Faq from "../../../components/Faq.astro";
import Provenance from "../../../components/Provenance.astro";
import { articleSchema, faqSchema, SITE, SITE_NAME } from "../../../lib/schema";
import { jevBuilds, JEV_BUILDS_READ_ON } from "@repo/lib/jev-builds";
import { ecosystem as layaEcosystem } from "@repo/lib/laya";

const title = "Open Source Jev: Substitutes, Replicas and What They Give Up (2026)";
const description =
  "Three different things get called open-source Jev: local substitutes, small replicas and training recipes. What the ones we actually read publish about themselves, including the head-to-head where the open model loses by 24 points.";
const canonical = `${SITE}/typesafe-jev/open-source`;
const READ_ON = "2026-09-23";

const bySlug = (slug: string) => jevBuilds.find((b) => b.dossier?.slug === slug);

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Is there an open-source Jev?",
    answer:
      "Not a release of the model. TypeSafe's Jev is a hosted API. What exists are three different things with the same name in the headline: local substitutes trained from scratch, small replicas built to study the architecture, and projects that turn another open model into a decision endpoint with the same calling shape.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the closest local substitute?",
    answer:
      "Laya, by Convai Innovations: 421M parameters, Apache-2.0, and an HTTP server that answers TypeSafe's own wire protocol, so a Jev client can be pointed at it. We ran that experiment and wrote it up under /laya. Von, by wfzyx, is the other candidate: a 395M-parameter model that publishes its own head-to-head against Jev — 72.0% macro on a 49-task suite against 96.6%, and 9.00 kills against 5.62 in ViZDoom.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I just call a small open model instead?",
    answer:
      "That is what featherless-ai/simple-jev does: it turns an open model into a classifier endpoint with the same request shape. It is the cheapest way to test the pattern without a TypeSafe key, and it does not carry the guarantee the hosted model makes — a fixed option set cannot be left.",
  },
];

/* Every row is a repository we opened; the numbers are the publishers' own. */
const substitutes = [
  /*
   * Laya is the row that changed what this page says. It is not a replica of the
   * architecture or an adapter around an open model — it is a from-scratch
   * decision model whose server speaks the same protocol, which makes it the only
   * entry here that could actually stand in for the hosted one in a deployment.
   * It has its own topic (/laya), so the row links there rather than to a build
   * dossier; the rest of this table is still dossiers from the builds column.
   */
  {
    href: "/laya",
    name: "Laya",
    stars: `${layaEcosystem.stars.toLocaleString("en-US")} ★`,
    what: "an open decision model with the same three question types — 421M parameters, Apache-2.0, runs on a CPU",
    gives: "a server that answers TypeSafe's `/v1/systemone`, so a client written for Jev works after a URL change — our own Jev client returned valid answers for all thirteen of our adversarial cases",
    givesUp: "a bundled encoder adapted for decisions rather than trained from scratch, and a published benchmark table whose Jev column the publisher never measured",
  },
  {
    slug: "wfzyx-von",
    name: "Von",
    what: "an open non-autoregressive decision model — 395M parameters, 1.5GB, runs locally",
    gives: "publishes its own head-to-head against the model it replaces: 72.0% macro on a 49-task suite vs 96.6%, and 9.00 kills vs 5.62 in ViZDoom",
    givesUp: "24 points of accuracy for a real-time loop it wins at 25 ms a decision",
  },
  {
    slug: "jaredpalmer-kev",
    name: "Kev",
    what: "a family of Jev-like decision models on Qwen3.5 — 0.8B, 4B and 9B — with the training path included",
    gives: "you own the model and the weights; measured on Kev-4B on an L4 in bf16, 118 ms for a 101-token request",
    givesUp: "you also own the training, the serving and the calibration",
  },
  {
    slug: "theoleecj-semif",
    name: "SemIf",
    what: "semantic ifs from open models, on a single 3090 at home",
    gives: "the boundary drawn in your own code, with an open model behind it",
    givesUp: "consumer hardware and your own judgement about the boundary",
  },
  {
    slug: "tianyucodings-nanojev",
    name: "NanoJev",
    what: "a small replica of the architecture: parallel decisions, dynamic option sets",
    gives: "the clearest read of how the thing works — a replica is an explanation",
    givesUp: "it is a teaching implementation, not a service",
  },
  {
    slug: "featherless-ai-simple-jev",
    name: "simple-jev",
    what: "turns any open model into a classifier endpoint with the same calling shape",
    gives: "the pattern without the vendor: testable today, at zero cost",
    givesUp: "the option-set guarantee is a prompt and a parser here, not a property of the model",
  },
].map((row) => ({ ...row, build: row.slug ? bySlug(row.slug) : undefined })).filter((row) => row.href || row.build);
---

<System title={`${title} | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={description} canonical={canonical} noReferrer>
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={articleSchema({ title, description, publishedAt: READ_ON, updatedAt: READ_ON, url: canonical })} />
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={faqSchema(faqs)} />

  <Breadcrumbs
    schema={true}
    items={[
      { name: "Jev", url: `${SITE}/typesafe-jev` },
      { name: "Open source", url: canonical },
    ]}
  />

  <article class="col">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="card-eyebrow">Jev · open source</p>
      <h1>What "open-source Jev" actually means</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        TypeSafe has not released the model. Several different kinds of project get written up
        under that headline anyway, and they answer different questions — "can I run this", "can I
        train it", "how does it work", "can I test the pattern without a key".
      </p>

      {/* The page cover (contract §5): one image, about this page's subject, with the
          meaning in the alt text and no caption announcing what it is. Placed after the
          opening paragraph rather than between the title and the first line, which is the
          one position the contract rules out. Dimensions are the file's own — the generator
          returns a square and the wide frame is composed before it is written, so the HTML
          no longer declares 16:9 over a 1:1 picture. */}
      <figure class="figure cover">
        <img src="/img/cover-jev-open-source.webp" alt="An engraving: a sealed wooden box on a small pedestal, surrounded by four opened copies of it on the bench, each showing one internal part missing." width="1024" height="576" decoding="async" />
      </figure>
    </header>

    <Callout eyebrow="In short">
      <li><strong>No open release of the model.</strong> Jev is a hosted API; everything below is somebody else's build.</li>
      <li><strong>The closest substitute publishes its own losing score:</strong> 72.0% vs 96.6% macro, and it wins the real-time loop at 9.00 vs 5.62 kills.</li>
      <li><strong>You can own the whole stack</strong> — Kev is a Jev-shaped family on Qwen3.5, 0.8B to 9B, with the training path included.</li>
      <li><strong>You can test the pattern today</strong> without a TypeSafe key by giving an open model the same request shape.</li>
      <li><strong>None of them promises what the hosted model promises:</strong> an answer that cannot leave your option set.</li>
    </Callout>

    <section>
      <h2>Four questions, four kinds of project</h2>
      <p>
        Sorting them by the question they answer is the only way this stops being confusing, because
        these projects are not competing with each other — they are competing with different parts
        of your problem.
      </p>
      <figure class="figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 236" width="100%" role="img" style="display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
          <title>A map of what gets called open-source Jev: local substitutes, model families you can train, small replicas that explain the architecture, and adapters that give an open model the same calling shape.</title>
          <text x="0" y="12" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">THE QUESTION YOU ARE ASKING</text>
          <text x="754" y="12" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)" text-anchor="end">WHAT ANSWERS IT</text>

          <text x="0" y="50" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">Can I run a decision model on my own box?</text>
          <rect x="470" y="34" width="290" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-sunken)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
          <text x="484" y="54" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-muted)">Von · 395M, 1.5GB · SemIf · a 3090</text>

          <text x="0" y="96" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">Can I own the model and keep training it?</text>
          <rect x="470" y="80" width="290" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-sunken)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
          <text x="484" y="100" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-muted)">Kev · 0.8B / 4B / 9B on Qwen3.5</text>

          <text x="0" y="142" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">How does the thing actually work?</text>
          <rect x="470" y="126" width="290" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-sunken)" stroke="var(--field-border)" />
          <text x="484" y="146" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-muted)">NanoJev · a replica as an explanation</text>

          <text x="0" y="188" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="14" fill="var(--text)">Can I test the pattern without a key?</text>
          <rect x="470" y="172" width="290" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-sunken)" stroke="var(--accent)" />
          <text x="484" y="192" font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-muted)">simple-jev · an open model, same shape</text>

          <line x1="0" y1="212" x2="760" y2="212" stroke="var(--field-border)" stroke-width="1" />
          <text x="0" y="230" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">Only the last one is a drop-in for the calling shape. None of them is a drop-in for the guarantee.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption style="margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); max-width: var(--measure);">
          The left column is the question a person arrives with; the right column is what actually
          answers it. Two of the four answers are not a Jev substitute at all, which is the confusion
          this page exists to clear up.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The one that stops being a substitute and becomes a swap</h2>
      <p>
        Every project above reproduces the shape of a decision model. One of them reproduces the
        <em>interface</em>: <a href="/laya">Laya</a> ships an HTTP server that answers TypeSafe's own{" "}
        <code>/v1/systemone</code>, which means the client you already wrote can be pointed at it and
        will keep working. We tested exactly that, and it holds further than we expected — our Jev
        probe returned a valid answer for all thirteen of its adversarial cases against the local
        server, with no code change beyond the endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also holds further than the claim needs it to. Sending the same thirteen cases to both
        models, Laya agreed with Jev's label on <strong>eleven of thirteen</strong>, and neither ever
        answered outside the option set. That is not a proof that one replaces the other — the
        thirteen cases are adversarial probes, not a workload — but it is a different kind of result
        from "a small model that does something similar", and it is the reason this page's answer to
        "what is the closest substitute" changed on 2026-09-24. The full account, including the
        twelve things that are not about accuracy, is under <a href="/laya/vs-jev">Laya vs Jev</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="quoted">
        What it does not reproduce is the guarantee as a property of the service. Both models kept
        inside the option set on our cases; only one of them promises it in a contract. A self-hosted
        server is also a thing you now have to run, patch and watch, which is the trade this page has
        been about since its first line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The five we read, and what each gives up</h2>
      <p>
        Every row is a repository we opened on {JEV_BUILDS_READ_ON}; the numbers are the publishers'
        own, and where a project publishes a head-to-head against the model it replaces, the losing
        side is printed too.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Project</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it gives up</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {substitutes.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <a href={row.href ?? `/typesafe-jev/builds/${row.build!.dossier!.slug}`}>{row.name}</a>
                  <div class="cell-note">{row.stars ?? (row.build ? `${row.build.stars.toLocaleString("en-US")} ★` : "")}</div>
                </td>
                <td>{row.what}</td>
                <td>{row.gives}</td>
                <td>{row.givesUp}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Von is the honest one, and that is why it is the useful one</h2>
      <p>
        The nearest thing to a substitute publishes the comparison that makes it look worse. On a
        49-task suite it reports <strong>72.0% macro against Jev's 96.6%</strong>, and in the ViZDoom
        Defend-the-Center protocol <strong>9.00 kills against 5.62</strong> — a 24-point accuracy gap
        and a real-time win, in one table, from the people who built it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the shape of the trade, and it is worth more than a benchmark somebody else ran: the
        open model is fast and cheap enough to sit in a loop, and it is wrong more often in ways that
        matter. Whether that is acceptable is a property of your loop. In a game the 395M model
        plays at 25 ms a decision and nobody is billed for a mistake; in a payment routing decision
        the same gap is not a trade, it is a defect.
      </p>
      <p class="quoted">
        Both sides of Von's comparison are the publisher's, including the number that flatters the
        hosted model. We have not reproduced either.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The thing none of them copies</h2>

      <figure class="figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 220" width="100%" role="img" style="display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
          <title>A scatter of the two published numbers that matter: the open substitute at 72.0% macro accuracy and around 25 ms per decision, against the hosted model at 96.6% and 474 ms measured on the same suite's reported protocol.</title>
          <text x="0" y="12" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">PUBLISHED ACCURACY AGAINST DECISION TIME</text>
          <line x1="70" y1="24" x2="70" y2="180" stroke="var(--field-border)" stroke-width="1" />
          <line x1="70" y1="180" x2="740" y2="180" stroke="var(--field-border)" stroke-width="1" />
          <text x="8" y="34" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)">100%</text>
          <text x="14" y="176" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)">60%</text>
          <text x="70" y="198" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)">fast</text>
          <text x="700" y="198" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)" text-anchor="end">slow</text>
          <text x="70" y="216" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-faint)">Left is better; up is better. Neither point is ours — both are the publishers'.</text>
          <circle cx="120" cy="132" r="7" fill="var(--text-faint)" />
          <text x="134" y="132" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text)">Von · 72.0% · ~25 ms</text>
          <circle cx="600" cy="46" r="7" fill="var(--accent)" />
          <text x="470" y="40" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="12" fill="var(--text)">Jev · 96.6% · 474 ms</text>
          <text x="470" y="58" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-faint)">time measured by a different party</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption style="margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); max-width: var(--measure);">
          Two points, two evaluation suites, two publishers — so the distance between them is the
          distance between two claims rather than the result of one test anybody ran on both.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Every project here reproduces the shape: a state goes in, an option comes out, in one pass.
        What none of them can reproduce from outside is the guarantee that made the hosted model
        worth writing about — that the answer <em>cannot</em> be something you did not offer. In{" "}
        <a href="/typesafe-jev/builds/featherless-ai-simple-jev">simple-jev</a> that guarantee is a
        prompt and a parser; in a replica it is whatever the evaluation showed.
      </p>
      <p>
        We tried to break the hosted model's version of it and could not, in thirteen attempts
        across four runs — and we published every attempt, including the two that tried to widen the
        answer set itself. So the honest summary of this page: if you need the guarantee you are
        buying a service and these projects are how you learn what it does; if you need the shape,
        several of them will give it to you this afternoon, and one will tell you where it fails.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this page cannot tell you</h2>
      <p>
        It cannot rank these projects, because they were not measured on the same tasks — one
        publishes a 49-task suite, another publishes latency on an L4, a third publishes nothing but
        a README. It cannot tell you which is cheaper over a month, since the honest comparison is
        your tokens against your electricity and your weekend. And it leaves out the forks that exist
        only as a description: a repository we cannot read is not a project, and this page is built
        from repositories we opened.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Faq items={faqs} />

    <Provenance
      source="repository READMEs"
      verified={JEV_BUILDS_READ_ON}
      extra={[
        "Each project links to its own page, where the dossier says what we checked and what we did not.",
        "Scores are the publishers' own; we reproduced none of them.",
      ]}
    />
  </article>
</System>

<style>
  .cell-note { margin-top: 2px; font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text-faint); }
</style>
